<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Commagene</h1><p class="st-meta">163 BCE – 72 CE · capital Samosata</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Commagene, a small kingdom on the upper Euphrates whose king Antiochus I built the colossal god-statues on the summit of Nemrut Dağı.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Commagene, the former <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kummuh">Kummuh</a>, lay between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taurus-mountains">Taurus</a> mountains and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>. Its governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaeus-of-commagene">Ptolemaeus</a> broke away from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> c. 163 BCE. His successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-i-callinicus">Mithridates I Callinicus</a> married a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid</a> princess, uniting Persian and Macedonian royal lines.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-i-theos-of-commagene">Antiochus I Theos</a> ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samosata">Samosata</a> and devised a royal cult in which Greek and Persian gods were merged. On <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-nemrut">Nemrut Dağı</a> he built a burial mound flanked by giant statues of himself with Zeus-Oromasdes, Apollo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithra">Mithras</a> and Heracles-Artagnes. Commagene grew rich from its fertile valleys and river crossings.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> annexed Commagene in 17 CE, restored it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iv-epiphanes">Antiochus IV</a> in 38 CE, and finally absorbed it into the province of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a> in 72 CE under Vespasian.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-commagene.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Commagene" width="320" height="188"><figcaption>Image: User:QuartierLatin1968 modified an image created by User:Cplakidas based on an original image by User:Sémhur from pub... · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_East_50_CE_Commagene_highlighted-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>163 BCE – 72 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Samosata</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mithridates I Callinicus, Antiochus I Theos, Antiochus IV</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greco-Persian royal cult</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Greek, Aramaic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Commagene</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-46&amp;focus=kingdom-of-commagene" aria-label="Kingdom of Commagene on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5641 826 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Commagene lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5961 1082L6140 1232M5961 1082L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6140 1232h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5961 1082h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5961 1082h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Samosata. <a href="/atlas?year=-46&amp;focus=kingdom-of-commagene">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
